WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.700
Choose your product line, and know how much you can really make.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.120
This is the video for module one of Handmade Accessories for Sale.

3
00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.450
A hobby maker makes whatever she feels like that day.

4
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:19.780
A seller makes a product line: a small group of items she can make again and again, at the same quality.

5
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.670
And she makes them for a customer she understands.

6
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:31.360
Today you will choose your line, set up a safe workspace, and work out how many pieces you can honestly promise.

7
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:38.930
The course uses four accessories: ankara bags, fabric earrings, headbands and beaded bracelets.

8
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:46.430
Cut from the same fabric, they sell as a set. A customer who came for earrings may leave with a matching headband.

9
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.390
You waste less too. Tote bag offcuts become headbands, and small scraps become earrings.

10
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.830
And repeating a few designs makes you faster and your quality consistent. That brings customers back.

11
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:12.110
Beautiful pieces still fail if they are made for no one in particular. So describe your buyer. First, her age range, for example 20 to 35.

12
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.260
Her style: bold prints, or neat for the office.

13
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.690
Her budget: what she already pays for similar items.

14
00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:27.080
Where she shops: WhatsApp, Instagram, campus, markets, church or mosque fairs.

15
00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:32.060
And what she buys it for: everyday use, owambe, or gifts.

16
00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:45.770
For example, Funmi in Ibadan was making everything, and her sales were small. She listed what people asked for in three months: tote bags 6 times, earrings 9 times, aprons once.

17
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:52.720
Most buyers were office women aged about 25 to 40 who attend many weekend events.

18
00:01:53.970 --> 00:02:00.170
She picked a lined ankara tote, covered-button earrings and a padded headband, all from one fabric.

19
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.990
She checked similar prices at Bodija market and on two Instagram shops.

20
00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:13.210
She dropped pillows and aprons but kept the patterns. Two or three core products is enough to start.

21
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:24.940
Accessory making uses heat, blades, glue and tiny parts. Rest irons and glue guns on a stand, never on fabric, and unplug them when you leave the table.

22
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:34.680
Cut away from your body, and keep blades and needles in a closed box. Use strong glues only with good air flow, away from flames.

23
00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:46.170
Beads, buttons and jump rings are choking hazards for young children. Work out of their reach, sweep after each session, and never sell beaded jewellery as a toy.

24
00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:56.630
Production capacity is how many pieces you can make in a week at normal quality, with real life included: children, work and power cuts.

25
00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.620
Step one. Make three pieces, timing each from start to finish.

26
00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.370
Step two. Average the times. Ignore the first if it was much slower.

27
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.140
Step three. Count the hours you can honestly spend each week.

28
00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.630
Step four. Divide those minutes by your time per piece.

29
00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.850
Step five. Take away about one fifth for set-up, mistakes and interruptions.

30
00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:31.400
For example, Halima in Kano timed three pairs of covered-button earrings: 28, 22 and 20 minutes.

31
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:36.640
She ignored the slow first pair, so her average is 21 minutes.

32
00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:42.520
She plans 6 hours a week for earrings. That is 360 minutes.

33
00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.310
360 divided by 21 is about 17 pairs.

34
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:58.560
Remove one fifth, and she can safely promise about 13 pairs a week. For 40 pairs for a wedding, she needs about three weeks, or help.

35
00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:11.270
For a bulk order, divide the quantity by your weekly capacity, add a few days, and give that date. Ask a deposit, commonly half, and write everything in one WhatsApp message.

36
00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:19.940
The common mistake is saying yes to avoid saying no. One late wedding order can cost you many future referrals.

37
00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:26.850
To remember. Make a few repeatable items for one clear customer, cut from one fabric.

38
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.060
Describe your buyer. Keep heat tools on stands and small parts away from children.

39
00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.890
And measure capacity with a timed trial, minus one fifth.

40
00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:45.560
Now write your product line card, price your materials list, photograph your tidy workspace, and time three pieces to find your capacity.

41
00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:47.860
Continue with the lessons below.
